[12 Regression] Wrong code at -O2 for well-defined infinite loop sincer8-5245
int foo(void) {
        int counter = 0;
        while (1) {
                if(counter >= 2)
                        continue;
                printf("%i\n", counter++);
        }
        return 0;
}

This is actually from a reddit post I came across:https://www.reddit.com/r/C_Programming/comments/yugk1y/gcc_optimization_behavior/There are theories in the comments above about UB due to integer overflow or non-terminating loops, but I'm pretty sure they don't apply (correct me if I'm wrong).

The controlling expression of the loop is a constant expression, so it can't be assumed to terminate, and the loop should repeat infinitely with `counter == 2`, so no overflow should occur. Instead, part of the loop is presumably DCEd, so control continues outside of the function.

Versions before gcc-13 had slightly different incorrect behaviours occur when replacing `2` in the condition with `3`, `4`, `5`, etc. These larger numbers appear to behave correctly now.

